Give patch.f fixed array extents, and tell HYBSVD about them.

patch.f declares six F90 automatic arrays sized by its dummy arguments ipoints and iterms:

    rv1(ipoints), pp(ipoints,iterms), pdat(ipoints,6), pwrk(iterms),
    pre(ipoints,iterms), z(ipoints,ipoints)

FORTRAN 77 has no automatic arrays, so f2c rejects the file and the build stubs it. patch is
reachable -- zienzhu.f is its only caller, and that is the Zienkiewicz-Zhu error estimator --
and the deployed module has it compiled (tools/ccx-stub-diff.py), so this is a real gap
against production rather than a cosmetic one.

Bounding alone would have been WRONG, and this is the interesting part. The file calls

    call hybsvd(ipoints,ipoints,ipoints,ipoints,ipoints,
   &            ipoints,iterms,pp,pwrk,matu,pp,matv,pre,z,pdat,nrhs,info,rv1)

and hybsvd's signature is

    SUBROUTINE HYBSVD(NA,NU,NV,NZ,NB,M,N,A,W,MATU,U,MATV,V,Z,B,IRHS,IERR,RV1)
    REAL*8 A(NA,1), U(NU,1), V(NV,1), Z(NZ,1), B(NB,IRHS)

The first five arguments are the DECLARED leading dimensions; M and N are the actual sizes.
Upstream passes ipoints for both because the arrays are declared with it. Re-declare them with
a fixed bound and leave the call alone, and every index hybsvd computes is wrong -- silently,
in a least-squares fit, which is the failure mode this project has already decided is worse
than an abort. So the leading dimensions become maxpt while M and N stay ipoints and iterms.

The bounds are read off the source rather than chosen:

  maxtrm=20   zienzhu.f sets iterms to exactly 4, 10, 11, 17 or 20.
  maxpt=4000  ipoints accumulates 1, 8, 8 or 27 per element in the patch around one node.

z is declared z(ipoints,ipoints) upstream, which would be 128 MB at maxpt. It does not need to
be square: hybsvd's own header says Z need only be "OF SIZE AT LEAST MIN(M,N)**2", and here
min(M,N) is iterms. z(maxpt,maxtrm) is larger than the algorithm can address, not smaller. The
whole set costs about 2.1 MB.

Both bounds carry a guard that stops the run and names the limit.

Generated with `diff -u` against the real 2.22 file.

--- a/src/patch.f
+++ b/src/patch.f
@@ -29,11 +29,27 @@
      & iterms,k,iflag,nrhs,info,node,ipnt,
      & mi(*),irow,ipoints,members(*),linpatch,ielidx,iavflag
 !
+C
+C     wasm: rv1/pp/pdat/pwrk/pre/z are F90 automatic arrays, sized by the dummy
+C     arguments ipoints and iterms. FORTRAN 77 -- all f2c implements -- has no such
+C     thing, so they take fixed extents here.
+C
+C     maxtrm=20 is not a guess: zienzhu.f is patch's only caller and sets iterms to
+C     exactly 4, 10, 11, 17 or 20.
+C
+C     z is declared z(ipoints,ipoints) upstream, which would be 128 MB at maxpt. It does
+C     not need to be square: hybsvd's own header says Z need only be "OF SIZE AT LEAST
+C     MIN(M,N)**2", and here M=ipoints, N=iterms, so min(M,N) is iterms. z(maxpt,maxtrm)
+C     is therefore larger than the algorithm can use, not smaller.
+C
+      integer maxpt,maxtrm
+      parameter (maxpt=4000,maxtrm=20)
+!
       real*8 xl(3,20),co(3,*),shp(4,20),
      & pgauss(3),pol(20),pntdist,w,scpav(6,*),xsj,
-     & sti(6,mi(1),*),xi,et,ze,rv1(ipoints),pp(ipoints,iterms),
-     & pdat(ipoints,6),pfit(6),pwrk(iterms),pre(ipoints,iterms),
-     & z(ipoints,ipoints)
+     & sti(6,mi(1),*),xi,et,ze,rv1(maxpt),pp(maxpt,maxtrm),
+     & pdat(maxpt,6),pfit(6),pwrk(maxtrm),pre(maxpt,maxtrm),
+     & z(maxpt,maxtrm)
 !
       real*8 tmpstr(6),gauss3d5e(3,4)
 !
@@ -43,6 +59,22 @@
 !
       include 'gauss.f'
 !
+C
+C     Stop rather than overrun. Both bounds are generous -- a patch is the elements
+C     around one node -- but a bound that is silently exceeded is the one failure mode
+C     worth refusing outright in a solver.
+C
+      if(ipoints.gt.maxpt) then
+         write(*,*) '*ERROR in patch: ipoints exceeds maxpt'
+         write(*,*) '       (WebAssembly build array bound)'
+         call exit(201)
+      endif
+      if(iterms.gt.maxtrm) then
+         write(*,*) '*ERROR in patch: iterms exceeds maxtrm'
+         write(*,*) '       (WebAssembly build array bound)'
+         call exit(201)
+      endif
+!
 !     initialize
 !
 !     iavflag: if 1, then build average of integration point values
@@ -194,7 +226,14 @@
          matv=.true.
          nrhs=6
 !
